oducts companies for any company I  think you empower women and you actually  recharge the world in the last few  months few weeks magazines ranging from  the Economist to Businessweek have cited  so many studies that show direct  correlation the good regression actually  between women's and women empowerment  and GDP growth around the world between  women empowerment and business success  between women empowerment and health and  sustainability between women empowerment  and also improved human health across  the world so if you take the community  social family all implications are vast  positive and vast there's no question  also that women actually impact the  public opinion inside the home you know  we at coca-cola something you know one  of the things that we do a lot is do a  lot of research about our consumers  about how they think how what's on their  mind what excites them what inspires  them and basically women determine we  know women determine what comes into the  home we know that globally seventy  percent of our shoppers are women of the  1.6 billion servings that we consumers  invite our products into their lives  every day seventy percent of the of  those decisions are taken by women and  we know therefore that we cannot grow  our business unless the people the  700,000 people that work for the  coca-cola system globally in 206  countries are representative of those  numbers and they are not representative  and that's why it big  a very important and a priority for me  to close that bridge and we know that  that's a journey so based on that  premise that no business can grow  successfully unless they close that  bridge and also all the growth based on  the fact that all the growth projections  we've been hearing about for the coming  years for China for Africa for India for  North and South America none of it is  really going to be possible without  women's economic empowerment more  success in women's economic empowerment  we also talked in coca-cola a lot about  a billion new people coming into the  middle class over the next decade a  billion new people coming into the  middle class over the next decade the  only way that the world and that's going  to add another twenty trillion to the  world in terms of richness the only way  that's going to happen the only way more  nations are going to rise out of poverty  the only way that the same trends that  China was able to successfully achieve  over the last ten years in terms of  bringing people out of poverty is only  going to be possible if more women are  empowered successfully and I think again  by the same token if we fail at that  certainly that growth is not going to  take place certainly those 1 billion  people are not going to come out and  join the middle class we still see too  many roadblocks to women's empowerment  what are those cultural roadblocks  educational roadblocks political  roadblocks financial roadblocks  technology roadblocks recently I had a  great great conversation with our  presidents ambassador-at-large for  global global woman issues moline  verveer ambassador of every year she  said something very that's resonated  with me a lot she said too many quote  too many of the best business ideas died  in bank  parking lots she said unquote and again  she was referring to the financial  roadblocks when she mentioned when she  was quoted saying that so what I'd like  to do over the next few minutes is  outlined three concrete ways that I  believe both business government and  also academia can have in terms of a  significant impact in generating female  empowerment around the globe and I think  my hope is that you know you think that  these ideas regardless of whether your  career takes you into business  government academic or non-profit  pursuits I think can be applicable I  think though one of the criteria  important criteria is that there has to  be better cooperation amongst three key  stakeholders for this to happen that is  government business and civil society we  need I call it the Golden Triangle I  think we need more corporation  collaboration concrete collaboration  between government business and civil  society for the things that i am going  to outline to enable the things that i'm  going to outline so let me just say what  those are number one the first way we  can help fuel women's empowerment i  think is the most obvious that is  accelerating women's leadership within  our own four walls I mean you can't sit  here talk about the importance of this  unless you're doing something inside  your own four walls I think one of the  most fulfilling programs I am personally  involved with at coca-cola is the  Women's Leadership Council that we  formed about three and a half years ago  it rotates every two years we have about  15 16 women leaders on this council the  chairman of this council currently cathy  waldor who's the global controller of  coca-cola is right here with me sure  raise your head  every number we put out talk about our  business she's behind that number and  when she's behind that number i'm  comfortable because i don't go to jail  and she is also she also heads up the  Women's Leadership Council right now for  the past year and a half very  successfully and what what this council  does is it acts as mentors it identifies  mid-level women leaders brings them in  develops them tracks their success and  and make sure that the senior leadership  of the company is always appraised of  new opportunities in this area and also  certainly we keep track of metrics in  how we're doing at senior levels  mid-level management and also recruiting  women leaders into the company so  recruit develop train and ensure that we  can promote and retain those are the I  think one of the things that we also  have to do better as a as a goal in  ensuring that we can be more successful  in women leadership is ensuring that we  have across all geographies a good  work-life balance that's one of the 