 at that both at  senior ranks mid-level management and  also entry level and so it that piece of  women leadership is mission critical and  that's why  we had a lot of debate when I when we  were designing that mission two years  ago when I brought all my bottling  partners from all around the world  together into Florida to craft and  design that vision which we call 2020  now and I specifically ensured that  women leadership was written on that one  page in that vision statement not about  equality not about in more broad words  women leadership and the keen insights  woman bring to our business there for  you know once again for us are profound  and again that was the message that i  took to Davos earlier this year at the  World Economic Forum when I appeared on  a gender parity panel discussion with  Arianna Huffington and also facebook CEO  Sheryl Sandberg the doubt that panel was  broadcast then at on CNBC I actually  would like to share just a very short  clip with our audience of that panel  then in Davos this year in January  sahaja I think it's all about  understanding of what's going to work  for your business in the next 10 to 20  years and really drive value if you can  push through a cultural change that  makes your company understand that  there's going to be half a billion new  women middle class and coming into the  middle class in the next 10 to 15 years  in the world and you need to communicate  understand better matched their  expectations and therefore you need  really a much more effective result in  terms of women leadership in your  organization across the world and that  is sustainability a quota will never  sustain you I think it's really  important you know goals not quotas as a  woman in the workforce some 40 years old  I hope to be in the workforce a long  time I never want to get a job because  they have to have me I want to get a job  because I earn it and quotas naked  quoted quotas do that I think goals are  different and I think the really  important thing and once again excited  about what coca-cola's doing is putting  communication on the table that was  about a sort of an hour long panel but I  just wanted you to get a glimpse of it  was a lively discussion and another  theme that was actually echoed in that  discussion was the importance of  bringing more women owned businesses  into our supply chains into our global  supply chains as companies which is I  think the second area important area  where again that triangle government  business civil society I think can make  some pretty impactful change positive  change because of the global reach  because of the influence of our  operations worldwide companies like us  are powerful agents of constructive  change one of the most exciting I think  women's entrepreneurial development  programs that i have been involved in so  far at coca-cola is our micro  distribution center network in africa  this program of micro distribution  center network allows us to identify and  support independent entrepreneurs to set  up distribution centers on behalf of our  business but they don't exclusively  distribute coca-cola products Micro  distribution centers are typically  located in Africa in areas where there  there's a lack of infrastructure lack of  stable roads and it makes us it very  difficult for us to get to to distribute  our products with normal vehicles and  normal distribution means and this  independent network of distributors they  just start distributing coca-cola's  beverage products  to retailers often by push carts or  bicycles because no other vehicles can  actually negotiate the infrastructure  that exists in place and in fact the  vast majority of our sales today after  about seven eight years of us investing  in this entrepreneurial system and  project in Kenya Tanzania Uganda  Ethiopia opia Mozambique and some other  countries vast majority of our business  is distributed through this network now  we have in total now about three and a  half thousand of these entrepreneurial  projects in operation in Africa  employing more than 20,000 people the  most important piece however is that a  thousand of these are owned and operated  by women I made a commitment again at  the CGI clinton global initiative that  we would at least ensure that at least  half of them half of these would be  women going forward and we've been very  successful the metrics we've achieved  and surpassed our goals in that area and  and it's it's beautiful to see how how  successful some of these women  entrepreneurs have become all across  Africa much more successful than  actually their male counterparts and  their connection how the world works is  so fascinating through our water  sustainability programs in Africa where  we take clean water to certain villages  in Africa which don't have clean  hygienic water and in Africa remember  that lack of clean water kills more  people than malaria and HIV combined so  we threw water replenishing projects we  call it rain replenish Africa initiative  we bring clean water to some villages  water harvesting project will harvest  rain  we captured the rain that normally comes  but it's not cat is not captured we have  irrigation projects and so on and so  forth women then become emancipated  because it's the women who you are  carrying water from 10 miles away to  their village clean water from another  town 10 miles away once women become  emancipated we then go in and try to get  a break down some of the other barriers  for women entrepreneurship and  empowerment like financial barriers  cultural barriers give them training at  basic accounting training give them  merchandising training stock rotation  training and they become fascinatingly  successful in in we now have these  projects in more than 11 countries in  Africa just one example you know one of  her one of these entrepreneurs rosemary  nigiri down and down in downtown nairobi  running one of these micro distribution  center